<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Limburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1065 CE – 1795 CE (approx.) · capital Limbourg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small duchy in the hills east of the Meuse whose succession war ended at the battle of Worringen in 1288, binding it to Brabant for five centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limbourg">Limbourg</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vesdre">Vesdre</a> was built in the early 11th century, and around 1065 Walram Udo of the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arlon">Arlon</a> became count of Limburg. His son Henry I claimed the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Lower Lorraine</a>, and later rulers kept the ducal title for their own lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Limburg was a land of pasture, woods and later cloth and lead mining around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verviers">Verviers</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vesdre">Vesdre</a> valley. Its dukes, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waleran-iii-duke-of-limburg">Waleran III</a>, intermarried with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> nobility. When the line ended in 1283, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-duke-of-brabant">John I of Brabant</a> fought for the inheritance and won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Worringen</a> in 1288.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Limburg was then ruled with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant</a>, passing to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-netherlands">Spanish Netherlands</a>. In 1661 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-overmaas">Overmaas</a> lands were divided with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the rest in 1795.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 1065 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">France and the Low Countries</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:80.90%;width:14.53%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1065 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Limbourg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry I of Limburg, Waleran III of Limburg, John I of Brabant</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French and Limburgish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Belgium, Netherlands</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Limburg</dd></dl></aside></div>

